{
  "note": [
    "The per-package coverage floors. Written by `tools/covercheck -write-baseline` from a real run, and read by the same tool as a gate.",
    "",
    "These are floors, not targets. A package below its floor fails the build. A package above its floor plus the margin also fails, and asks for the floor to be raised — a floor that only fails downwards is a floor somebody lowers once and everything above it is free afterwards.",
    "",
    "They are statement coverage from `go test -coverprofile`, not branch coverage: `go test` does not produce branch coverage, and a metric this repository cannot measure with a flag is a metric it reports without measuring.",
    "",
    "The floors hold for the build modes in `build`. Coverage is not a property of the source: internal/migrate measures 78.6% without -race and 71.4% with it on an unchanged tree, so a floor from one mode is not a floor for the other, and `check` reports that it could not measure rather than comparing numbers that do not belong together.",
    "",
    "`cmd/`, `migrations/` and `tools/` are named in `excluded` and are not floored. `migrations` is SQL with a Python harness beside it and no Go statements, and a floor of zero for a directory with no code teaches a reader to ignore this file.",
    "",
    "`internal/roadmapcheck` was added on 2026-10-12 from the Windows measurement, not from a Linux run: the package is platform-neutral — no build-tagged files and no platform-skipped tests — so the statement set and the number are the same on both. A Linux run will prove it or correct it, which is what the margin is for.",
    "",
    "`internal/sitemodel`'s 82 carries the same provenance. The C3 adjacency tests are platform-neutral and the package has no build-tagged files, so the Windows measurement of 82.1 is the Linux number too; recorded on 2026-10-13 because leaving the old floor below the new measurement is the failure mode the margin exists to catch.",
    "`internal/audit` carries the same provenance as `internal/roadmapcheck`: its floor is the Windows measurement of 2026-10-21, not from a Linux run. The package is platform-neutral -- no build-tagged files and no platform-skipped tests -- so the statement set and the number are the same on both. A Linux run will prove it or correct it, which is what the margin is for. `internal/truthcheck`, `internal/migrationreadme` and `internal/operatormetrics` carry the same provenance: their floors are the Windows measurement of 2026-10-21, not from a Linux run. Both packages are platform-neutral -- no build-tagged files and no platform-skipped tests -- so the statement set and the number are the same on both. A Linux run will prove it or correct it, which is what the margin is for.",
    "Fourteen entries were carried across from the WINDOWS measurement on 2026-10-21: internal/aistate, internal/calibration, internal/classify, internal/embedaudit, internal/embeddings, internal/emulator, internal/eventsearch, internal/investigate, internal/nlp, internal/ortdetect, internal/policy, internal/proposal and internal/sidecar and internal/youtube were not in this file at all, and a package that is not in the floors is a package the gate cannot measure, which it reports as RuleUnknown.",
    "They are the WINDOW numbers, and they are marked as such rather than left to look like a Linux measurement. Re-measure them on Linux and the numbers will move."
  ],
